Ranking #1 in the UK for “hair transplant Turkey.”

This page was designed with Visual Semantics and written according to Algorithmic Authorship rules.

When we create a page, we technically follow four steps:

1. Visualization
2. Verbalization
3. Commercialization
4. Contextualization

For the hair transplant industry, the most important attributes are clear:

Before-after results. Cost. Procedure steps. Hair transplant types. Customer reviews. Doctor and expert validation.

These attributes should not exist separately.

They should be connected, multiplied, and structured across the entire page experience.

Because a page doesn’t rank only because it has text.

It ranks because its visual, verbal, commercial, and contextual signals support the same search intent from multiple angles.

There was also an entitization problem with this brand.

Its authority was diluted across three different domains. I used the Knowledge Graph API to understand whether these domains were sharing ranking signals for the same brand.

Then, we moved the two extra domains into the main domain to consolidate the ranking signals.

After consolidation, the same documents started to perform better.

